 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Somebody has broken the side mirror on Barinov's car, and his ex-wife Eleonora is the first to point it out. He doesn't take it well. Hear it in Russian first.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Виктор Петрович, у вас тут зеркало сломанное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Да надо же!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А вы не заметили?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Что у вас тоже?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "У меня? Как? Где?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ну вот же.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Теперь мы квиты.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Костя, ты видел?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Он разбивает зеркало её машины.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "А она типа не при делах.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я уверена.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Это просто так не закончится.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Back to the start, and then on to what Nastya makes of it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "Виктор Петрович, у вас тут зеркало сломанное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Viktor Petrovich, you have a broken mirror here.' Let us break down this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "у",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "вас",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "тут",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is courteous Russian for 'you have'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "сломанное",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " puts a special ending on 'broken' to go with 'mirror'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Да надо же!",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, would you look at that!' Let us inspect this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "надо",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "же",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is an old idiom meaning 'well I never!' It's made out of the word for 'necessary', and the little ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "же",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " that adds feeling.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "А вы не заметили?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Didn't you notice?' Barinov keeps the polite ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "вы",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " with his ex-wife. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "заметили",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", noticed, ends in the past ending that goes with polite 'you' even for one person.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Что у вас тоже?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'That yours is too?' It is only 'at you also', which is Russian for 'yours too'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "тоже",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the part to take away.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "У меня? Как? Где?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Mine? How? Where?' Three question words fire in a row, with no action word between them. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "У",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "eleonora",
   "t": "меня",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is Russian for 'mine', and the other two are 'how' and 'where'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ну вот же.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Right there.' Three tiny words, ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Ну",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "вот",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "же",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", pile up to mean 'look, right there'. The little ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "же",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " adds the weight, and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "вот",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is the pointing word.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Теперь мы квиты.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Now we're even.' We can break down this part: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "квиты",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " reuses the term in the English 'quits', and it means the same. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Теперь",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'now', and ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "мы",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'we'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Костя, ты видел?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Kostya, did you see?' A man's ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "видел",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " takes a special ending. A woman's would end differently, and the question stays the same.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Он разбивает зеркало её машины.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'He smashes the mirror on her car.' The action word ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "разбивает",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means smashing to bits, and the everyday word for breaking is for a mechanism. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "её",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "машины",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " has a special ending that means 'of'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "А она типа не при делах.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And she acts like she had nothing to do with it.' Let's consider the speech: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "типа",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is casual speech for 'sort of' or 'like'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "не",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "при",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "делах",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", or 'not at the deals', is slang for 'nothing to do with it'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Я уверена.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I am sure.' The extra sound at the end of ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "уверена",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " tells you Nastya is speaking, since a man would say it without. Two words are all it takes.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "Это просто так не закончится.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'This won't just blow over.' Let's look at this line: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "просто",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "так",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is 'just like that'. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "nastya",
   "t": "закончится",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means 'will end', reflecting back because things end by themselves. And the whole scene without stopping.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
